    10039-dev-oci23-native-loginretry-realserver.oraproxy
                                     ociloginretry --attempts=wrongpassword,
                                      unknownuser,correctpassword through
                                      oraproxy against the real 12.2 server
                                      (oracle122centos7x64, service ora1),
                                      one OCIServerAttach, no detach or
                                      reattach between attempts - the #9311
                                      sequence. Native encoding: both ends
                                      are x86_64 Linux, so the real server's
                                      TTIPRO banner matches the client's own
                                      platform. This is the reference a
                                      modern (non-9i) client's login-retry
                                      byte shape is written against for
                                      #10039. [0013] is the first login
                                      attempt (wrong password); [0014]
                                      [0015] [0016] are the server's BREAK
                                      marker, its RESET marker, and the
                                      client's one RESET marker back -
                                      sendAuthenticationBreak()'s exchange;
                                      [0017] is the ORA-01017 error itself,
                                      an ordinary DATA packet; [0018] is the
                                      client's NEXT login attempt
                                      (unknownuser), also ordinary DATA -
                                      not a marker. The same five-packet
                                      shape repeats at [0020]-[0025] for the
                                      unknownuser failure, then
                                      correctpassword succeeds, completing
                                      at [0028], and the capture ends with a
                                      clean logoff at [0031]. The decisive
                                      finding: after reading the error, a
                                      real client never sends a second,
                                      post-error marker - it goes straight
                                      to its next login as ordinary DATA
                                      (#10039)
    10039-dev-oci23-portable-loginretry-sqlrelay-wrongerror.oraproxy
                                     the same ociloginretry sequence and
                                      the same real 12.2 backend, but
                                      through a local sqlr-listener
                                      (oracleprotocol, "SQLRelay/PortableTTC"
                                      banner - sqlrelay only ever negotiates
                                      portable) taken before the #10039 fix.
                                      Shows the bug this ticket is for:
                                      wrongpassword reports ORA-03120
                                      (two-task conversion routine: integer
                                      overflow), unknownuser reports
                                      ORA-24327 (explicitly attach before
                                      authenticating a user), and
                                      correctpassword afterward fails too
                                      with ORA-24327, even though the
                                      server's own debug=protocols trace
                                      confirms it sent ORA-01017 every time.
                                      Root cause, from the byte diff against
                                      samples/oracle112-login-expiredpwd-
                                      break.cap's portable ORA-28001 packet:
                                      sendErrorPacket()'s portable branch
                                      sent putOci7Summary()'s field layout
                                      (one field shorter, with the sequence
                                      number frozen at 3) to every client,
                                      but a modern client reads putSummary()
                                      's layout, so every field past the
                                      third is shifted and misread. The
                                      capture also shows the resulting
                                      symptom on the wire: the client sends
                                      an extra post-error marker at [0018],
                                      unanswered in the real-server capture,
                                      which sendMarkerCancelError() answers
                                      at [0019]-[0020] - a marker/response
                                      pair that exists only because of the
                                      misparse (#10039)
    10039-dev-oci23-portable-loginretry-sqlrelay-fixed.oraproxy
                                     the same sequence again, same instance,
                                      captured after sendErrorPacket() was
                                      changed to send putSummary()'s field
                                      layout - with the login's own sequence
                                      number instead of a frozen 3 - to any
                                      client that isn't a 9i one. All four
                                      attempts now report correctly:
                                      wrongpassword and unknownuser
                                      ORA-01017, emptypassword ORA-01005,
                                      correctpassword succeeds. The packet
                                      sequence now matches
                                      10039-dev-oci23-native-loginretry-
                                      realserver.oraproxy one for one - 31
                                      packets on both sides, break/reset/
                                      client-reset in front of each failure,
                                      the error as ordinary DATA, the
                                      client's next login as ordinary DATA
                                      with no extra marker, and a successful
                                      login completing at [0028] (#10039)

Each of the 9808-*, 9810-*, 9976-* and 9700-*.oraproxy captures above is
oraproxy's own capture format (see "oraproxy" below), taken by interposing
oraproxy locally on the client host and pointing the client's tnsnames.ora
at it, rather than a raw tcpdump - a real NIC's MSS is too small for
oradecode's one-frame-one-TNS-chunk assumption to hold. oradecode reads them
with no conversion.

Every column in these four multicol-*.cap captures is a NUMBER column
defined SQLT_STR (the legacy putField() character-array arm) - only the
column count and fetch mode vary. They evidence the legacy fetch path's
row/column framing across column counts, not any additional Oracle
datatype; see #9704 for datatype coverage itself.

The three real Oracle test servers on this network
--------------------------------------------------

Not discoverable anywhere else in this tree:

    oracle122centos7x64.firstworks.com   10.0.2.1:1521   Oracle 12.2
    oracle112centos5x64.firstworks.com   10.0.2.2:1521   Oracle 11.2
    oracle102centos4x86 (10.0.2.3)        10.0.2.3:1521   Oracle 10.2

The first two take user testuser / password testpassword against SID
ora1 (see oracle.java). As of this writing, the 11.2 server's testuser
account has an expired password (ORA-28001) - that needs a DBA to reset
it before a full login can be captured there again; it is unrelated to
oraproxy or oradecode, both of which handle the error path correctly
regardless.

The third, oracle102, is the only one of the three old enough to speak
OCI7/O3LOGON - it's the backend the oracleprotocoloci7 test instance
uses (see test/sqlrelay.conf.d/oracleprotocol.conf.in). Its hostname is
not recorded anywhere in this tree beyond this line; only its IP,
10.0.2.3, is confirmed (#9664 comment 21).
